 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Yes. Eating out in Melbourne costs more than it did a year ago, and prices are still rising faster than inflation overall.\n\n**Latest figures (ABS monthly CPI, \"meals out and take away foods\")**\n- **August 2026:** Melbourne prices were **3.5% higher than in August 2025**. The national figure was 4.1%.\n- **Over the past 12 months:** Melbourne's annual rate stayed between about 3.3% and 4.4%. It peaked at 4.4% in May 2026 and was 4.1% in July 2026.\n- **Index level:** The Melbourne index rose from 99.82 in August 2025 to 103.29 in August 2026. The index is set to 100 in September 2025.\n\n**Compared with overall inflation in Melbourne**\n- Meals out rose from 95.40 in April 2024 to 103.29 in August 2026, a gain of about 8.3%.\n- The all-groups CPI rose from 96.51 to 103.04 over the same months, about 6.8%.\n- Restaurant and takeaway prices have therefore risen a little faster than prices generally.\n\n**Trend**\n- Annual meals-out inflation in Melbourne was high in early 2024: 5.8% in the March quarter and 5.0% in the June quarter.\n- It slowed to about 2.3–2.6% through the first half of 2025.\n- It has since picked up again to the mid-3% range.\n- August 2026 was lower than July's 4.1%, so the recent pace is easing slightly.\n\nMelbourne has been slightly cheaper to eat out in, in terms of price growth, than the national average for most of the past year. These figures show how fast prices are rising. They don't show whether Melbourne is cheaper or dearer than other cities in dollar terms.",
